            <abstract><p>This work comprises a set of five miracles (the first miracle is included in the introduction) preceded by an introduction and completed by an epilogue (the epilogue may be absent in some manuscript witnesses; 
                it is present in <ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/BNFet116">Paris, Bibliothèque nationale de France, BnF Éthiopien 116</ref>).  
                As stated in the introduction, the miracles pertain to the feast of the Commemoration of Christ’s Death—a celebration that gained popularity among Christians thanks to 
                <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS6418Mabaas">Mabāʾa Ṣǝyon</persName> and his <ref type="work" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/LIT1475GadlaM">Vita</ref>. 
                The introduction also mentions a certain priest Yoḥannǝs, who began celebrating the commemoration in the city of Ṭāmo, in the region known as ʾAnāmo, 
                following the example of <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS6418Mabaas">Mabāʾa Ṣǝyon</persName>. 
                Of the three witnesses identified so far, the <ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/BNFet116">Paris, Bibliothèque nationale de France, BnF Éthiopien 116</ref> is the most legible, thanks to its clear and fine handwriting. 
                The other two witnesses,  <ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/ESmma010">Bǝḥerāwi Kǝllǝlāwi Mangǝśti Tǝgrāy, Maʿago ʾAmānuʾel, MMA-010</ref> and <ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/ESgbi013">Gāntā ʾAfašum, Gʷāḥgot ʾIyasus, GBI-013</ref> are readable but present some difficulties 
                due to the relatively poor condition of the parchment and poorly formed handwriting.
            </p>
            </abstract>
